<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Juan Velasco Alvarado</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1910">1910</a>–<a href="/years/1977">1977</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Juan Velasco Alvarado was a Peruvian general who seized power in a coup in October 1968 and ruled as president until other officers removed him in 1975. His Revolutionary Government of the Armed Forces nationalised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-petroleum-company">International Petroleum Company</a> and other foreign firms, carried out one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>'s most sweeping agrarian reforms, and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> an official language. Economic crisis and his failing health led to his overthrow.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juan-velasco-alvarado.jpg" alt="Juan Velasco Alvarado" width="320" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 144155804@N02 (Flickr) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Juan_F._Velasco_Alvarado.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Juan Velasco was born in Castilla, a city near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piura">Piura</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>'s north coast, the son of Manuel José Velasco, a medical assistant, and Clara Luz Alvarado, who had 11 children. He described his youth as one of &quot;dignified poverty&quot;, working as a shoeshine boy in Piura. In 1929 he stowed away on a ship to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, falsified his age and joined the army as a private on 5 April 1929. A year later he scored highest of all applicants in the entrance exam of the Escuela Militar de Chorrillos, and in 1934 he graduated at the head of his class.</p><p>During Fernando Belaúnde's administration (1963–1968) Congress censured several cabinets, and political instability was widely perceived. Officers who had put down two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> uprisings inspired by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuban-revolution">Cuban Revolution</a> came to regard social inequality as a danger to national security. A dispute with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-petroleum-company">International Petroleum Company</a> over the La Brea y Pariñas oil fields became a scandal when a key page of a contract was found missing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Velasco seized power on 3 October 1968 in a bloodless coup, closed Congress and sent Belaúnde into exile. The army took over the disputed oil fields on 8 October. The new Revolutionary Government of the Armed Forces pursued a programme known as Peruanismo, nationalising industries from fisheries to mining and telecommunications. In its first ten years the government expropriated 15,000 properties, some nine million hectares, benefiting about 300,000 families, in what was the second-largest land reform in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin American</a> history after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>'s.</p><p>The education reform of 1972 provided for bilingual schooling for indigenous peoples, and in 1975 a law made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> an official language equal to Spanish, though it was never enforced. In foreign policy Velasco was hostile to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, tightened relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and the Soviet bloc and bought large quantities of Soviet arms. On 29 August 1975 military commanders began a coup in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacna">Tacna</a>, known as the Tacnazo, and appointed Prime Minister Francisco Morales Bermúdez president. Velasco, seriously ill for at least a year, announced that he would not resist, saying that Peruvians cannot fight against each other.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Velasco kept a low profile until his death in 1977, and a large crowd of sympathisers attended his funeral. The success of his economic policies is still debated. After the agrarian reform people moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> and other coastal cities, where the failure to manage the influx contributed to slums.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Juan Velasco Alvarado” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>General <b>Juan Velasco Alvarado</b> seized power in 1968 and carried out a radical programme: nationalisation of the International Petroleum Company, a sweeping agrarian reform and official recognition of Quechua.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-peru">Republic of Peru · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>By the mid-1970s most of Latin America was ruled by officers: Brazil from 1964 to 1985, Uruguay from 1973 to 1985, Chile from 1973 to 1990 and Argentina from 1976 to 1983, while in Peru the reformist government of General <b>Juan Velasco Alvarado</b> redistributed land.</blockquote><a href="/government/military-junta">Military junta · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Castilla District, in what is now <a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-5.2,-80.62&amp;year=1953" aria-label="Castilla District on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2315 2358 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Juan Velasco Alvarado"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2765 2628h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2765 2628h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-5.2,-80.62&amp;year=1953">What was here in 1953? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
